The code statements that lead to the bug are:
if (inst->state == IRIS_INST_ERROR)
	goto exit;
exit:
	if (ret) {
		iris_helper_buffers_done(inst, q->type, VB2_BUF_STATE_ERROR);
		iris_inst_change_state(inst, IRIS_INST_ERROR);
	}
Reason:
When the instance is in `IRIS_INST_ERROR`, the early `goto exit` skips the entire stop streaming procedure, including the call to `iris_session_streamoff()` and the buffer completion in the exit block. This leaves buffers unreturned to vb2 (causing warning messages in the vb2 core) and prevents proper teardown of the firmware session. Additionally, `iris_helper_buffers_done` is only called if `ret` is non-zero, so even when the early check is removed, a successful stop streaming would also skip buffer completion—this is fixed by moving the call outside the conditional.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
- (removal of the following line)
  if (inst->state == IRIS_INST_ERROR) goto exit;
exit:
	iris_helper_buffers_done(inst, q->type, VB2_BUF_STATE_ERROR);
	if (ret)
		iris_inst_change_state(inst, IRIS_INST_ERROR);
Reason:
Removing the early error-state check ensures that `stop_streaming` always proceeds to complete all pending buffers via `iris_helper_buffers_done()` and run any necessary cleanup, even if the instance is already in `IRIS_INST_ERROR`. Moving the buffer completion outside the `if (ret)` block guarantees that buffers are returned to vb2 regardless of whether `iris_session_streamoff()` succeeds or fails. The state transition to `IRIS_INST_ERROR` is now only performed when a new error occurs during the stop streaming process, which is the correct behavior for robust session teardown.

